    "result_text": "All 29 tests pass, including the new `Cp18Tests`. The build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the per-city capacity rule: creating an owner is rejected with **409 Conflict** when the owner's city already contains 50 or more owners.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`CityAtCapacityException.java`** (new) \u2014 a `RuntimeException` in the `rest.advice` package, mirroring the existing `DuplicateTelephoneException`/`DuplicateHouseholdException` 409 exceptions, carrying the rejected city as its rejected value.\n\n2. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 in `addOwner`, added a call to a new `rejectCityAtCapacity(...)` guard right after address normalization. The guard counts existing owners whose city matches case-insensitively (treating `null` as empty, consistent with the existing `assignCustomerCode` logic) and throws `CityAtCapacityException` when the count is `>= 50` (`CITY_CAPACITY` constant).\n\n3. **`ExceptionControllerAdvice.java`** \u2014 added an `@ExceptionHandler` for `CityAtCapacityException` that returns a 409 `ProblemDetail`, following the exact shape used by the other conflict handlers (`errors: [\"city\"]` plus a `schemaValidationErrors` entry).\n\nThe check runs before the household/telephone duplicate checks and before persistence, so an at-capacity city is rejected cleanly. The acceptance test (`Cp18Tests`) fills a fresh unique city to exactly 50 and confirms the 51st is rejected \u2014 it passes, and no existing tests regressed.",
